   "text": {
    "L1": "Magadha became powerful because geography, resources and ambitious rulers worked together.",
    "L2": "Fertile Ganga plains produced food, rivers helped transport and defence, iron made strong tools and weapons, and forests supplied elephants for the army.",
    "L3": "Chandragupta Maurya, guided by Chanakya, defeated the Nandas around 321 BCE and founded the Mauryan empire.",
    "L4": "From Pataliputra, the empire was governed through provinces, officials, tax collectors, a large army and spies.",
    "L5": "The order is Alexander, Chandragupta, Bindusara, then the Kalinga war.",
    "L6": "After the bloody Kalinga war, Ashoka promoted dhamma: non-violence, respect, tolerance and kindness. Dhamma was not a new religion forced on everyone.",
    "L7": "Ashoka spread these ideas in edicts carved on rocks and pillars. Historians treat them as the ruler's point of view, not as complete truth.",
    "L8": "So resources helped Magadha rise, organisation held the empire together, and Ashoka changed his way of ruling after Kalinga."
   },
